  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday morning through next Thursday)\nIssued at 145 PM EST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nAn upper level low will move along the northeast coast at the \nbeginning of the long term. As it does, it will continue to funnel a \nsignificant amount of mid to upper level dry air southeastward over \nour area through Monday. By Tuesday, a moisture-starved shortwave \nwill move into the Arklatex region, which may bring an increase in \nscattered clouds by mid week, but precipitation chances will remain \nnear zero through at least Thursday. Overall, we can expect surface \nhigh pressure to keep our area dry and tranquil during the majority \nof the long term. \n\nBy the end of the period, another shortwave will traverse a broad \nupper level trough, with some disagreement in the guidance on the \ntiming and placement of any developing associated surface low. The \nmost favorable chance for precipitation will hold off at least until \nthe end of next week and hinge on whether it is able to develop \nenough to advect deeper Gulf moisture over the area. \n\nTemperatures will be within a few degrees of seasonal norms, with \nhighs ranging from the mid to upper 60s north of the Atlanta metro \narea to the low to mid 70s farther south. Morning lows will be the \ncoolest yet this fall, ranging from the mid to upper 30s to low 40s.",
